from pathlib import Path
import json,re,collections,difflib
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
changes=[]
for name in ['main.tex','supplement.tex']:(R/'revised'/name).write_bytes((R/'sources'/name).read_bytes())
def edit(name,start,new,defect):
 p=R/'revised'/name;s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8');begin=s.index(start);end=s.find('\n\n',begin);matches=[s[begin:end if end>=0 else len(s)]]
 if start=='Low-timestep conversion':matches=[s.split('\\begin{abstract}\n',1)[1].split('\n\\end{abstract}',1)[0]]
 if len(matches)!=1:raise ValueError((start,len(matches)))
 old=matches[0];s=s.replace(old,new,1);p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
 changes.append({'file':name,'location':start,'defect':defect,'before':old,'after':new})
edit('main.tex','Low-timestep conversion',r'''Activation and message reconstruction are common measures of low-timestep graph conversion, but they summarize only part of the information needed to preserve a classifier's decision. We examine the missing information through hard-event interventions in converted superpixel graph classifiers. Reducing the error of the actual graph message can worsen the downstream teacher-class margin: this occurs in 40.9\% and 31.9\% of message-improving interventions on MNIST and CIFAR10, respectively. Temporal codes with exactly the same local decoded output and event count can also produce different downstream predictions and activity. Directional margin analysis explains the first distinction, while a source-recurrence counterexample isolates the second. Three-teacher position interventions identify the first node activation as a major short-prefix bottleneck; matched calibration controls distinguish this sensitivity from a realizable correction. Residual centering further shows how a representation change can reduce a projection-activity proxy while leaving decision preservation to be evaluated separately. These findings characterize the spatial direction and temporal history lost by reconstruction metrics and motivate evaluating finite-event graph conversion through downstream decisions, required prefixes, and whole-network activity.''','Rhetorical self-questioning and repeated result announcements')
edit('main.tex','Graph neural networks make predictions',r'''Graph neural networks predict by repeatedly mixing information over a graph. Encoding their continuous representations with a small number of events changes both the propagated values and the histories received by later layers. In visual graphs, a perturbation at one superpixel can cross a downstream quantization boundary and change the image-level decision even as average reconstruction error decreases.''','Clause-stacked motivation')
edit('main.tex','ANN-to-SNN conversion offers',r'''ANN-to-SNN conversion approximates a pretrained network without retraining its weights. Calibration reduces activation mismatch, bias correction compensates temporal error, and differential coding represents changes rather than repeatedly transmitting rates~\cite{li2021,wu2024ftbc,huang2025}. These techniques support high fidelity at short prefixes. In our graph adaptations, four active ticks approximately preserve the accuracy of three independently trained teachers on both fixed and gated superpixel networks. The shortest prefix, however, leaves a fidelity gap. This regime allows us to test which aspects of the converted computation reconstruction metrics capture.''','Template-like closing question and vague transition')
edit('main.tex','The distinction is sharper',r'''Two forms of information loss are relevant. Next-message error uses the actual adjacency and weights, yet its norm discards the perturbation's direction relative to class boundaries. Exact local terminal reconstruction preserves the decoded value, but omits the intermediate states encountered by downstream event units. A correction can therefore reduce message error while moving the final decision toward a competing class; a code can match its local endpoint while changing the downstream trajectory.''','Slogan-like comparison and repeated distinction')
edit('main.tex','We investigate both obstacles',r'''We test these distinctions through controlled hard-event interventions in frozen graph classifiers. The primary setting is MNIST and CIFAR10 superpixel classification with residual GCNs. A complementary GatedGCN study examines residual centering, while classical node classification supplies context for propagation and readout effects. Prediction agreement measures fidelity to the teacher; ground-truth accuracy measures task performance. We likewise distinguish local event counts from whole-network cost.''','Inventory ending lacks explicit scientific roles')
edit('main.tex','The resulting contribution is',r'''Together, the analyses characterize the information required for decision preservation at low temporal budgets. Directional class margins connect graph-propagated errors to classification, and exact local codeword equivalences isolate the effect of temporal history. Calibration and representation controls then test whether these diagnostics translate into useful finite-event corrections. The resulting evaluation principle is concrete: assess downstream decisions at every required prefix and account for the whole-network activity induced by each realizable intervention.''','Repeated contribution announcement and abstract-noun padding')
edit('main.tex','Layerwise calibration adjusts',r'''Layerwise calibration uses pretrained ANN responses to adjust converted networks~\cite{li2021}. Distribution-based threshold and potential initialization~\cite{bojkovic2024} and forward temporal bias correction~\cite{wu2024ftbc} address systematic approximation errors. Initial-potential fitting and bias compensation are therefore established calibration components. Retaining the final membrane residual preserves information lost by integer spike counts; this readout distinction underlies our early node experiments separating hidden approximation from output quantization.''','Defensive novelty disclaimers interrupt literature synthesis')
edit('main.tex','Differential coding with graded',r'''Differential coding with graded units and spiking neurons (DCGS) transmits changes in represented activation and supplies a threshold-iteration procedure~\cite{huang2025}. Its public event unit provides the basis for the graph adaptations studied here. One-Spike SNN demonstrates GCN conversion~\cite{hwang2025onespike}, and temporal-misalignment research establishes the importance of event history~\cite{bojkovic2025}. Against these precedents, our temporal experiment isolates a specific question: whether exact terminal equality at a local site preserves downstream decisions and total activity under the same finite event alphabet.''','Repeated negative novelty framing')
edit('main.tex','Degree-Quant studies',r'''Degree-Quant studies quantization-aware graph training, and A$^2$Q incorporates aggregation into quantization design~\cite{degreequant2021,a2q2023}. Both account for structural effects on approximation. We examine a related issue in a frozen converted computation: the relationship between immediate message fidelity and the final decision. Feature-only and permuted-operator controls isolate the role of the actual propagation operator while preserving the task graph and fitting budget. The visual models and data follow Benchmarking GNNs~\cite{dwivedi2023}. Static propagation, feature-dependent gating, and classical node classification use different operators and codes and are analyzed separately.''','Rhetorical self-question and repeated claim advertisement')
edit('main.tex','The same mismatch appears',r'''Finite residual allocation exhibits the same distinction. Compared with local allocation, actual-message allocation reduces normalized message MSE from 0.001378 to 0.001280 on MNIST and from 0.002018 to 0.001717 on CIFAR10. On CIFAR10, 491 of 512 graphs improve this metric, yet 27 of those change from correct to incorrect. The corresponding MNIST counts are 440 and 17. Gains on other graphs can offset these losses in aggregate accuracy, so an improved mean can coexist with adverse individual decisions. These policies share extra event opportunities; their realized event counts can differ.''','Conversational result commentary and redundant qualifier')
edit('main.tex','The exchange changes the first-prefix',r'''The exchange changes the first-prefix signal delivered to later layers. Their nonlinear event states integrate that signal before local equality is restored, so the terminal hidden tensor alone does not summarize the downstream temporal computation. For the same reason, lowering the first site's absolute event amplitude need not lower total events, amplitude, or graph delivery activity. The scalar counterexample isolates this state dependence, and the graph experiment shows its consequences in the residual network.''','Mechanical repeated contrast and redundant closing')
edit('main.tex','Our experiments identify two ways',r'''The experiments expose two distinct information losses in reconstruction-based evaluation. Message norms omit the signed direction of an error relative to competing classes, while terminal reconstruction omits the temporal states traversed by downstream units. Position recovery locates influential sites; hard-event changes show that improving a local metric can worsen the decision; exact codeword exchanges demonstrate that eliminating local terminal error still leaves downstream temporal ambiguity.''','Repeated contribution summary and formulaic parallelism')
edit('main.tex','These observations suggest a disciplined',r'''A useful design criterion follows from these distinctions. Specify the downstream quantity preserved by an approximation, then evaluate a realizable finite-event change under fixed task computation. Measure teacher agreement and accuracy separately, and check every required prefix alongside total activity. Residual centering preserves an affine representation identity and reduces a projection proxy, whereas task-aware calibration changes decisions without a consistent advantage in the evaluated settings. Each intervention is informative at the level its evidence supports.''','Prescriptive checklist rhythm and vague assurance')
edit('main.tex','The empirical scope is',r'''The evidence concerns superpixel classification with fixed residual GCNs, complemented by hybrid GatedGCN representation studies and historical node experiments. The core hard-event and temporal tests use one teacher and previously used validation or training graphs. Their scope motivates replication across tasks, direct One-Spike comparisons, and a modulewise analysis of residual and event-boundary effects. Feature-dependent GatedGCN gates require a separate perturbation analysis, and changing input topology lies outside the experiments. Within these conditions, spatial message fidelity and local temporal equality each leave information relevant to the final decision unspecified. Evaluating that information is essential to interpreting low-timestep graph conversion.''','Stacked limitation inventory with repeated conclusion')
edit('supplement.tex','The main text connects frozen',r'''The evidence addresses a common question across several graph models and event codes. Table~\ref{tab:scope} distinguishes the role and scope of each experiment. All official visual validation and test splits were used during earlier development, so their endpoint evaluations are descriptive even when the evaluated parameters had been frozen. The manuscript reuses the recorded results and predictions; no new training or test inference was performed for this preparation.''','Manuscript-production framing and defensive negative contrast')
edit('supplement.tex','The historical audit repaired',r'''The retained historical results use corrected Planetoid row alignment, duplicate-edge handling, bias-calibration masks, and accumulation of actual hidden events. Superseded values are excluded. The analysis also distinguishes symmetric normalization from row-stochastic normalization and treats saturated inputs separately in the hidden-error bound. Large readout differences, particularly on ogbn-arxiv, illustrate the importance of decoder semantics rather than establish a new SOTA result. Because the compact historical package lacks per-seed raw SNN predictions, these means provide contextual evidence with weaker provenance than the retained visual raw arrays.''','Bug-fix chronology presented as scientific narrative')
for start,replacements,defect in [
 ('At one tick, fixed-GCN',{'Which sites cause this gap?':'Position interventions localize this gap.','This locates a useful intervention point, but the recovery uses a continuous response and imposes no finite-event constraint.':'This recovery measures conditional sensitivity using a continuous response without finite-event constraints.'},'Rhetorical question and contrastive caveat'),
 ('The distinction also matters for event allocation.',{'Thus neither low $T$, local sparsity, nor the projected additions in Fig.~\\ref{fig:centered} is a complete efficiency claim.':'These measurements distinguish low $T$, local sparsity, and the projected additions in Fig.~\\ref{fig:centered} from complete inference cost.'},'Repeated negative-parallel efficiency disclaimer')]:
 s=(R/'revised/main.tex').read_text(encoding='utf-8');begin=s.index(start);old=s[begin:s.index('\n\n',begin)];new=old
 for a,b in replacements.items():new=new.replace(a,b)
 edit('main.tex',start,new,defect)
(R/'revision-log.json').write_text(json.dumps(changes,indent=2),encoding='utf-8')
for name in ['main.tex','supplement.tex']:
 before=(R/'sources'/name).read_text(encoding='utf-8');after=(R/'revised'/name).read_text(encoding='utf-8')
 (R/'audit'/(name+'.diff')).write_text(''.join(difflib.unified_diff(before.splitlines(True),after.splitlines(True),fromfile='original/'+name,tofile='revised/'+name)),encoding='utf-8')
def numeric(s):return collections.Counter(re.findall(r'(?<![A-Za-z])\d+(?:\.\d+)?',s))
assert numeric((R/'sources/main.tex').read_text())==numeric((R/'revised/main.tex').read_text()),'numeric tokens changed'
for name in ['main.tex','supplement.tex']:
 before=(R/'sources'/name).read_text(encoding='utf-8');after=(R/'revised'/name).read_text(encoding='utf-8')
 assert collections.Counter(re.findall(r'\$[^$]*\$',before))==collections.Counter(re.findall(r'\$[^$]*\$',after))
 for env in ['equation','table','table*','figure']:
  pattern=r'\\begin\{'+re.escape(env)+r'\}.*?\\end\{'+re.escape(env)+r'\}'
  assert re.findall(pattern,before,re.S)==re.findall(pattern,after,re.S),(name,env)
 assert re.findall(r'\\cite\{[^}]+\}',before)==re.findall(r'\\cite\{[^}]+\}',after)
before=(R/'sources/main.tex').read_text(encoding='utf-8');after=(R/'revised/main.tex').read_text(encoding='utf-8')
assert before.split('\\section*{LLM Usage Statement}')[1]==after.split('\\section*{LLM Usage Statement}')[1]
response={'manuscript':after,'notes':'Native primary-agent prose revision using the explicit writing contract and requested scientific-paper-writing guides. Named defects and before/after passages are recorded in revision-log.json. All numeric tokens, math, tables, figures, citation keys and the full disclosure were preserved.'}
(R/'writer-response.json').write_text(json.dumps(response,indent=2),encoding='utf-8')
print(f'Polished {len(changes)} passages; numeric, mathematical, citation, table, figure and disclosure checks passed')
